ous at $x=0$\n- $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$" :question/worked-solution "We're required to determine the left and right continuity properties of $h(x)$ at $x=0.$\n\nFirst, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(0) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The function is left-continuous at $x=0$ since the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}h(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-0) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡1 \\\\  & =0 \\\\  & =h(0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function is right-continuous at $x=0$ since the right-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}h(x) & =-2(0)^{2} \\\\  & =0 \\\\  & =h(0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function is both left and right-continuous at $x=0,$ which implies that the function is continuous at $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-141012/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "25229703-4cfb-5010-ae5c-f6a54503ced3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-141012"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-141017/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" :answer/id #uuid "1a68ee84-f277-5f76-b0f7-5fd8bacf3d63" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141017/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "e2b2124b-1e02-5c58-a206-c69e9b6a1ce6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141017/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "4769394d-5e24-5039-a70e-258f54912db8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141017/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "4507314f-efbb-593f-87fd-434bc3fab924" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141017/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "2d32fa25-5b27-54c1-8ddf-8de0467c9e71" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-141017/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "f11fa2e3-1823-583d-bd18-69e14423c663" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-141017/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer-field/choices ["q-141017/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" "q-141017/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-141017/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-141017/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" "q-141017/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-141017" :question/id #uuid "95829165-19c6-5259-bf84-c7e0a04f8a0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-141017" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $h(x),$ defined below?\n\n$$\nh(x)={\\begin{aligned}2x+1, & x<-1 \\\\ 1, & x=-1 \\\\ x+2, & x>-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $h(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=-1$\n- $h(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=-1$\n- $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1$" :question/worked-solution "We're required to determine the left and right continuity properties of $h(x)$ at $x=-1.$\n\nFirst, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-1)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. The function is *not* left-continuous at $x=-1$ since the left-sided limit is *not* equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{-}}h(x) & =2(-1)+1 \\\\  & =-2+1 \\\\  & =-1 \\\\  & \\ne h(-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function is right-continuous at $x=-1$ since the right-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}h(x) & =-1+2 \\\\  & =1 \\\\  & =h(-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For the function to be continuous at $x=-1,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is not left-continuous at $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-141017/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "25229703-4cfb-5010-ae5c-f6a54503ced3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-141017"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-9965"] :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $h(x),$ defined below?\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042135/assets/ba95d95494b67b2da7f5ebeb108fd75a42945291bae5440ff12d2edaa73f2798.png)\n\n- $h(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=1$\n\n- $h(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=1$\n\n- $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$" :question/worked-solution "We're required to determine the left and right continuity properties of $h(x)$ at $x = 1.$\n\nLet's start by computing $h(1){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(1) & ={e}^{1-1} \\\\  & ={e}^{0} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The function is left-continuous at $x=1$ since the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}h(x) & ={e}^{1-1} \\\\  & ={e}^{0} \\\\  & =1 \\\\  & =h(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function is *not* right-continuous at $x=1$ since the right-sided limit is *not* equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}h(x) & =1-1 \\\\  & =0 \\\\  & \\ne h(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStatement III is false. For the function to be continuous at $x=1,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous at this point. However, we have already determined that the function is not right-continuous at $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-9972"] :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f(x),$ defined below?\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042135/assets/4985f1b43abee44c927a619c1d8965c98a2283f65d5723429477f7e29dbdc74c.png)\n\n- $f(x)$ is left-continuous at $x = 0$\n\n- $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x = 0$\n\n- $f(x)$ is continuous at $x = 0$" :question/worked-solution "We're required to determine the left and right continuity properties of $f(x)$ at $x = 0.$\n\nFirst, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The function is left-continuous at $x = 0$ since the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & =2-0 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function is right-continuous at $x = 0$ since the right-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & =\\frac{4}{0+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2} \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & =f(0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function is both left and right-continuous at $x=0,$ which implies that the function is continuous at $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
